### What is the problem?

Given a string, find the minimum number of character deletions to make it a palindrome. For "abcda", removing 'b' and 'c' leaves "ada" which is a palindrome, so the answer is 2.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class MinDeletionsPalindrome {
    public static int minDeletions(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }
        return n - dp[0][n - 1];
    }
}
```

### Why does this reduce to the longest palindromic subsequence?

The characters we choose to keep must form a palindrome while maintaining their original order. That is exactly the definition of a palindromic subsequence. To delete as few as possible, we keep as many as possible, which means keeping the longest palindromic subsequence. The deletions are everything left over.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ReductionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCDA";
        int n = s.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)
                    ? 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]
                    : Math.max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }
        int lps = dp[0][n - 1];
        System.out.println("String: " + s + " (length " + n + ")");
        System.out.println("Longest palindromic subsequence: " + lps);
        System.out.println("Minimum deletions: " + (n - lps));
    }
}
```

```text
String: ABCDA (length 5)
Longest palindromic subsequence: 3
Minimum deletions: 2
```

### How does the LPS recurrence work?

The entry dp[i][j] holds the longest palindromic subsequence of the substring from i to j. When the ends match, both characters are part of the palindrome, so add 2 to the inner answer. When they do not match, one end must be dropped, so take the max of dropping the left end or the right end.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LPSRecurrenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "BACA";
        int n = s.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1];
                    System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "]=" + dp[i][j] + " (match '" + s.charAt(i) + "')");
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                    System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "]=" + dp[i][j] + " (mismatch)");
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("LPS: " + dp[0][n - 1] + ", Deletions: " + (n - dp[0][n - 1]));
    }
}
```

```text
dp[2][3]=2 (match 'A')
dp[1][2]=1 (mismatch)
dp[1][3]=2 (mismatch)
dp[0][1]=1 (mismatch)
dp[0][2]=1 (mismatch)
dp[0][3]=2 (mismatch)
LPS: 2, Deletions: 2
```

### Why does this also equal the minimum insertions?

Deleting an unmatched character and inserting its mirror on the other side are two symmetric ways to fix the same imbalance. Both operations reduce the problem by one unmatched character. So the minimum deletions to make a palindrome equals the minimum insertions to make a palindrome. Both are `n - LPS`.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class DeletionsEqualsInsertions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCDA";
        int deletions = MinDeletionsPalindrome.minDeletions(s);
        // Minimum insertions would also be 2
        System.out.println("Min deletions: " + deletions);
        System.out.println("Min insertions: " + deletions + " (same value)");
    }
}
```

```text
Min deletions: 2
Min insertions: 2 (same value)
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n squared) for filling the n-by-n table. Space is O(n squared) for the table. Space can be reduced to O(n) with a rolling two-row array since each row only depends on the row below it.

### What are the edge cases?

A string that is already a palindrome needs 0 deletions. A single character needs 0 deletions. An empty string needs 0 deletions. A string with all distinct characters needs n-1 deletions (keep any one character).

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MinDeletionsPalindrome.minDeletions("ABA"));   // 0
        System.out.println(MinDeletionsPalindrome.minDeletions("A"));     // 0
        System.out.println(MinDeletionsPalindrome.minDeletions("ABCD"));  // 3
    }
}
```

```text
0
0
3
```

### What are common mistakes?

Confusing this with the minimum deletions to make two strings equal (which uses LCS, not LPS). Using the substring palindrome DP (boolean table) instead of the subsequence DP (integer table). Forgetting to subtract from n at the end.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you reconstruct which characters to delete?
- How does this compare to the edit distance problem?
- Can you optimize space to O(n) with a rolling array?
- What if you can delete from only one end of the string?
